## Install

```sh
npm install {{.Name}}
```

No Go toolchain is needed to use this package: the compiled WebAssembly module
ships inside it. It runs in Node 20+ and in browsers via any bundler.

## Use

```ts
import { {{.FirstImport}} } from "{{.Name}}";

const result = await {{.FirstCall}}({{.FirstArgs}});
```
{{if .Multi}}
Each Go package has its own namespace, so two of them may both export `New`
without either being renamed. A namespace is also importable on its own, which
is what you want when you need one package and not the others:

```ts
import { {{.FirstFunc}} } from "{{.Name}}/{{.FirstNS}}";
```

One WebAssembly module serves every namespace, however you import them.
{{end}}
Every function returns a promise, because the Go program runs on its own
goroutine and must never block the JavaScript event loop. A Go `error` becomes
a rejected promise carrying a real `Error`:

```ts
import { GoError } from "{{.Name}}";

try {
  await {{.FirstCall}}({{.FirstArgs}});
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof GoError) console.error(err.message);
}
```

### Managing the instance yourself

The named exports share one lazily-booted instance, which is what you want most
of the time. When you need a custom asset location, several independent
instances, or deterministic teardown, create your own:

```ts
import { createClient } from "{{.Name}}";

const client = await createClient({
  wasmUrl: new URL("./main.wasm", import.meta.url),
});
try {
  await client.{{.FirstCall}}({{.FirstArgs}});
} finally {
  await client.dispose();
}
```

